// lg-paralegal — generated from src/lg-paralegal-*.js by tools/merge.js. Edit the src files, then run: node tools/merge.js
// Paralegal / Legal Assistant. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["lg-paralegal"] = (QB["lg-paralegal"] || []).concat([
["Role", "What does a paralegal do?", "**Why they ask:** the opening question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I support attorneys with substantive legal work: drafting documents and correspondence, legal research, managing case files and documents, organizing discovery, preparing for hearings or closings, filing with courts, and tracking deadlines. I do a lot of the work that keeps cases and matters running, under attorney supervision. The key boundary is that I can't give legal advice, represent clients in court, or practice law — but I'm deeply involved in the work that supports it.\"\n\nTip: Substantive support under supervision; can't give legal advice.", "B", "E", null, {"t":1,"f":["What can't a paralegal do?","Give legal advice, represent clients in court, set fees, or anything that constitutes practicing law — that's the attorney's role."]}],
["Ethics", "What's the line between paralegal work and the unauthorized practice of law?", "**Why they ask:** a critical ethical boundary.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I can do substantive legal work — research, drafting, document prep — but under attorney supervision, and I cannot give legal advice to clients, represent them in court, or set fees. If a client asks me a legal question, I don't answer it myself; I relay it to the attorney. Crossing into the unauthorized practice of law harms clients and the attorney's license, so I'm careful to stay on the right side of that line and clear about my role.\"\n\nTip: Never give legal advice; relay client legal questions to the attorney.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["A client asks you 'what should I do?' legally. What do you say?","That I can't give legal advice but I'll get their question to the attorney, and follow up to make sure it's answered."]}],
["Deadlines", "How do you manage deadlines and avoid missing one?", "**Why they ask:** missed deadlines can be malpractice.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I use a calendaring/docketing system religiously, calculate deadlines carefully (court rules on how to count days are tricky), build in buffers, set reminders ahead, and double-check critical dates. A missed filing deadline can lose a case and cause a malpractice claim, so this is one of the most important things I do. I'm meticulous and never rely on memory for a date that matters.\"\n\nTip: Docketing system, careful date calculation, buffers — never rely on memory.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why is calculating court deadlines tricky?","Rules vary on whether to count calendar or business days, when the clock starts, and how holidays and filing methods affect it."]}],
["Research", "How do you conduct legal research?", "**Why they ask:** a core skill.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I clarify the legal question with the attorney, search relevant statutes, regulations, and case law using tools like Westlaw or Lexis, verify the law is still good (not overturned), and summarize findings clearly and objectively. I cite accurately and note any contrary authority. Good research is thorough and honest — I flag law that cuts against us too, because the attorney needs the real picture, not just supportive results.\"\n\nTip: Verify law is current; flag contrary authority honestly.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why check if a case is still 'good law'?","It may have been overturned or limited by later rulings — citing bad law in a brief is a serious error."]}],
["Drafting", "What kinds of documents do you draft?", "**Why they ask:** practical skill.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Depending on the practice area: pleadings and motions, discovery requests and responses, contracts, correspondence, closing documents, and filings. I draft from templates and precedent, tailor to the matter, and ensure accuracy and completeness for attorney review. I'm careful with detail — in legal documents, a wrong name, date, or figure, or a missing clause, can have real consequences, so precision is everything.\"\n\nTip: Draft accurately from precedent for attorney review; precision matters.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why is precision so critical in legal drafting?","A small error — wrong party, date, or missing term — can change legal meaning or invalidate a document."]}],
["Discovery", "How do you manage discovery and document review?", "**Why they ask:** major litigation task.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I organize and track document production, help review and categorize documents for relevance and privilege, manage e-discovery platforms, prepare privilege logs, and keep everything organized and defensible. Discovery can involve huge volumes, so organization and attention to privilege are crucial — accidentally producing a privileged document is a serious mistake. I'm systematic and careful to protect the client's interests.\"\n\nTip: Organize, protect privilege, stay defensible.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What's privilege in discovery?","Protection that keeps confidential attorney-client communications and work product from having to be disclosed."]}],
["Organization", "How do you stay organized managing many matters at once?", "**Why they ask:** the role demands it.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Systems and discipline: a reliable case management system, prioritized task lists, calendared deadlines, well-organized files (physical and digital), and clear communication with attorneys about priorities and status. I handle many matters with competing deadlines, so I stay ahead, flag conflicts early, and never let things fall through the cracks. Being the organized, reliable one is a huge part of a paralegal's value.\"\n\nTip: Systems, prioritization, proactive communication.", "B", "E", null, {"f":["How do you prioritize competing deadlines?","By urgency and consequence — court deadlines and client-critical items first — and I flag conflicts to the attorney early."]}],
["Confidentiality", "How do you handle client confidentiality?", "**Why they ask:** fundamental legal duty.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I treat everything as strictly confidential — don't discuss matters outside work, secure files and communications, and follow the firm's data security practices. The attorney-client privilege and the duty of confidentiality are foundational to legal work, and a breach can harm the client and the matter. I'm bound by the same confidentiality expectations as the attorneys I support, and I take it seriously.\"\n\nTip: Strict confidentiality; same duty as the attorneys.", "B", "E", null, {"f":["Why does confidentiality matter so much in law?","Clients must be able to share everything with their lawyers; breaching it harms the client and can waive privilege."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a time you helped under pressure.", "**Why they ask:** real experience.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"A major filing deadline moved up unexpectedly. I reorganized my priorities, coordinated the document assembly, double-checked every exhibit and the deadline calculation, and got the attorney a complete, accurate package in time to file. Staying calm, organized and detail-focused under the time crunch meant we met the deadline cleanly with nothing missed.\"\n\nTip: Show composure, organization and accuracy under deadline pressure.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What did you double-check?","The deadline calculation, that all exhibits were complete and correct, and the filing requirements — no room for error."]}],
["Technology", "How do you use technology in paralegal work?", "**Why they ask:** modern practice.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Case and document management systems, e-discovery platforms, legal research tools (Westlaw, Lexis), court e-filing, and increasingly AI tools for document review and research assistance. I embrace tools that make the work more efficient and accurate, while verifying their output — AI can speed research and review but needs human checking, since an unverified AI-generated citation or summary can be wrong, which is a known risk in legal work.\"\n\nTip: Use tools for efficiency but verify, especially AI output.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's the risk of AI legal research?","It can produce plausible but fake or wrong citations — everything must be verified against actual sources."]}],
["Closing", "Why do you want to be a paralegal?", "**Why they ask:** motivation.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I like substantive, detail-oriented work that genuinely matters to clients' cases, the mix of research, writing and organization, and being an essential part of the legal team. I'm meticulous and reliable, which the role rewards, and I find the work intellectually engaging without needing to be the attorney out front.\"\n\nTip: Detail-oriented, substantive work; essential team role.", "B", "E", null, {"f":["What practice area interests you?","Tailor it — litigation, corporate, real estate, IP — and why it fits your strengths and interests."]}]
]);

// Paralegal / Legal Assistant — part 2. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["lg-paralegal"] = (QB["lg-paralegal"] || []).concat([
["Litigation", "Walk me through the stages of a civil litigation case.", "**Why they ask:** a litigation paralegal must know the whole lifecycle to support it.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It starts with pleadings — the complaint and the answer that frame the dispute. Then discovery, usually the longest phase, where both sides exchange information through document requests, interrogatories, depositions, and requests for admission. Along the way there may be motions, like a motion to dismiss or for summary judgment. If it doesn't settle — and most cases do — it goes to trial, followed potentially by post-trial motions and appeal. I support each stage differently: drafting and organizing pleadings, managing the discovery machine, preparing exhibits and witness materials for trial. Knowing where a case sits tells me what the attorney needs next and what deadline is coming.\"\n\nTip: Pleadings, discovery, motions, trial, post-trial/appeal — most cases settle; knowing the stage tells you the next deadline and deliverable.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Which phase is usually the most document-intensive?","Discovery — it's where the volume of document review, production, and deposition preparation lives, and where paralegals carry much of the load."]}],
["Research", "What is cite-checking and why does accuracy matter so much?", "**Why they ask:** cite-checking is a core, high-stakes paralegal task.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Cite-checking means verifying that every legal citation in a brief is accurate — correct case name, reporter, court, and year — properly formatted to the required citation style, and that the authority still says what it's cited for and is still good law, not overturned or questioned. I verify the proposition the citation supports actually appears in the source, and I use citator tools to confirm a case hasn't been reversed. It matters enormously because a brief with a wrong or dead citation undermines the firm's credibility with the court and can hurt the client's position — a court that catches a bad cite trusts the rest of the brief less.\"\n\nTip: Verify every citation is accurate, properly formatted, supports its proposition, and is still good law — a bad cite damages credibility with the court.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What does it mean to confirm a case is 'still good law'?","That it hasn't been reversed, overruled, or seriously questioned by later decisions — citator tools flag this so you don't rely on authority that no longer holds."]}],
["E-discovery", "What is e-discovery and how do you support it?", "**Why they ask:** electronic discovery is now central to litigation support.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"E-discovery is the discovery of electronically stored information — emails, documents, chats, databases. A common way to think about the workflow is identify, preserve, collect, process, review, and produce the relevant data. My role spans a lot of it: helping issue and track litigation holds so data isn't destroyed, coordinating collection, organizing documents in a review platform, running searches and setting up review protocols, building privilege logs, and preparing productions in the required format. The scale can be huge, so I use the platform's tools — search terms, deduplication, and increasingly analytics — to make review efficient while making sure nothing responsive or privileged is mishandled.\"\n\nTip: E-discovery runs identify-preserve-collect-process-review-produce; paralegals manage holds, review platforms, searches, privilege logs, and productions.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why is a litigation hold so important early in e-discovery?","It stops routine deletion of potentially relevant data — failing to preserve evidence can lead to serious sanctions against the client."]}],
["Discovery", "What is a privilege log and how do you prepare one?", "**Why they ask:** privilege logs are detailed, judgment-adjacent paralegal work.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"When documents are withheld from production because they're privileged — attorney-client communications or work product — you generally have to log them so the other side can assess the claim. The log describes each withheld document enough to justify the privilege — date, author, recipients, type, and the basis — without revealing the privileged content itself, which is the balance. I prepare it carefully from the review platform's coding, make sure the privilege calls were made or confirmed by an attorney, and keep it consistent and complete, because an inadequate or over-broad privilege log invites a challenge and possibly an order to produce. It's exacting work where accuracy and attorney oversight both matter.\"\n\nTip: Log each withheld document (date, author, recipients, basis) enough to justify privilege without revealing content — under attorney oversight of the calls.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why must an attorney confirm the privilege calls?","Determining what's actually privileged is a legal judgment; a paralegal organizes and documents it, but the attorney is responsible for the privilege determination."]}],
["Drafting", "How do you draft pleadings like a complaint or an answer?", "**Why they ask:** drafting under supervision is a central paralegal skill.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I work from the firm's templates, prior filings in similar matters, and the attorney's direction, then tailor them to the specific facts and claims. For a complaint that means setting out the parties, jurisdiction, the factual allegations, and the causes of action in properly numbered paragraphs; for an answer, responding to each allegation and asserting defenses. I make sure it follows the court's formatting and local rules exactly, the facts are accurate, and it's internally consistent. Then the attorney reviews and finalizes it, because the legal strategy and the final content are theirs — I produce an accurate, well-organized, rule-compliant draft that saves them time.\"\n\nTip: Draft from templates and attorney direction, follow court formatting and local rules exactly, ensure factual accuracy — the attorney finalizes strategy and content.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why follow the court's local rules so precisely?","Courts can reject or strike filings that don't meet formatting, length, or procedural requirements — a rule violation can delay the case or waive a position."]}],
["Procedure", "What do you need to know about filing documents with a court?", "**Why they ask:** correct filing is a procedural responsibility paralegals own.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I need to know each court's specific requirements, because they vary: filing deadlines and cutoff times, formatting and page limits, required cover sheets or certificates, filing fees, and the e-filing system that court uses. I confirm the document is complete and properly signed, file it before the deadline with buffer for technical problems, serve it on the other parties as required, and save the file-stamped confirmation. I never file at the last minute if I can avoid it, because an e-filing system glitch at 11:58 PM on a deadline is a nightmare. Procedural precision here protects the client — a missed or defective filing can have serious consequences.\"\n\nTip: Know each court's rules, deadlines, formats, fees, and e-filing system; file early with buffer, serve properly, and keep the stamped confirmation.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why avoid filing right at the deadline?","E-filing systems can glitch or go down, and a technical failure at the deadline can jeopardize the filing — a buffer protects against disaster."]}],
["Calendaring", "How do you manage a docketing or calendaring system for case deadlines?", "**Why they ask:** blown deadlines are a leading cause of malpractice; docketing is a paralegal safeguard.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Docketing is the disciplined tracking of every deadline — court dates, filing due dates, discovery cutoffs, statute-of-limitations dates, response deadlines — usually in a dedicated calendaring system, not just someone's memory. When a deadline comes in, I calculate it correctly using the applicable rules for counting days, enter it with reminders well in advance, and build in buffer time to actually prepare rather than scramble. I treat it as a safety system with redundancy, because a single missed deadline can forfeit a client's rights or claim. The reminders ladder up so nothing sneaks up, and critical dates get extra confirmation.\"\n\nTip: Track every deadline in a dedicated system, calculate dates by the applicable counting rules, set laddered advance reminders — it's a malpractice safeguard.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why is the statute of limitations date especially critical?","Missing it can permanently bar the client's claim entirely — it's one of the highest-stakes deadlines, so it gets extra confirmation and early reminders."]}],
["Trial", "How do you prepare for trial as a paralegal?", "**Why they ask:** trial prep is intense, organized paralegal work.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Trial prep is where organization pays off. I assemble and organize exhibits — marking, indexing, and preparing copies for the court, witnesses, and opposing counsel — build trial binders or their electronic equivalent so the attorney can find anything instantly, coordinate witness logistics and prepare witness files, prepare demonstratives, and put together the trial notebook with pleadings, motions, outlines, and key documents. During trial I track exhibits as they're admitted, take notes, manage documents in real time, and handle the constant logistics so the attorney can focus on the courtroom. The goal is that nothing the attorney reaches for is ever missing or slow to find.\"\n\nTip: Organize and index exhibits, build trial binders, coordinate witnesses and demonstratives, and manage documents live so the attorney finds anything instantly.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's your role with exhibits during the trial itself?","Tracking which exhibits are offered and admitted, keeping the exhibit list current, and having copies ready for the court, witness, and opposing counsel on demand."]}],
["Depositions", "How do you prepare for and summarize depositions?", "**Why they ask:** deposition support and summaries are high-value paralegal deliverables.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Before a deposition I help prepare: assembling documents the witness may be shown, organizing exhibits, preparing a witness file, and handling scheduling and the court reporter logistics. Afterward, a key deliverable is the deposition summary — condensing a long transcript into an organized, usually page-and-line-referenced summary of the key testimony, so the attorney can quickly find what a witness said without re-reading hundreds of pages. A good summary is accurate, neutral, and keyed to the testimony so the attorney can jump straight to the page and line. I flag important admissions or inconsistencies for the attorney's attention while keeping the summary itself factual.\"\n\nTip: Prep exhibits and witness files beforehand; afterward produce an accurate, page-and-line-referenced summary flagging key testimony and inconsistencies.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What makes a deposition summary useful to an attorney?","Accuracy, neutrality, and page/line references so the attorney can instantly locate testimony and rely on the summary without re-reading the full transcript."]}],
["Organization", "How do you set up and maintain a case file so anyone can find anything?", "**Why they ask:** systematic file organization is foundational paralegal competence.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I use a consistent structure across matters — standard folders for pleadings, correspondence, discovery, research, and key documents — whether physical or in the document-management system, so anyone on the team can navigate a file they've never opened. I name and date documents consistently, keep a chronology or key-document index for complex matters, and file things as they come in rather than letting a pile build. The test is whether a colleague covering for me, or the attorney at 9 PM before a hearing, can find what they need without me. Consistency beats cleverness — a predictable system everyone understands is worth more than a brilliant one only I can use.\"\n\nTip: Use a consistent folder structure, name and date documents consistently, index key documents, and file promptly — predictable beats clever.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why is consistency across matters more valuable than a custom system?","Anyone can step into a consistently organized file and find what they need — a bespoke system only you understand becomes a bottleneck when you're unavailable."]}],
["Administration", "How does timekeeping and billing work for a paralegal, and why does it matter?", "**Why they ask:** billable paralegals must track time accurately and ethically.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"In many firms paralegal time is billable to clients, so I record it in increments against the right matter with a clear, honest description of the work. It matters for accuracy — the client is paying and deserves truthful, specific entries, not vague padding — and because paralegal billing rules require the work to be substantive legal support, not clerical tasks, to be billable. I track time contemporaneously rather than reconstructing it days later, which is both more accurate and more honest. Good time entries also help the attorney understand where effort went on a matter. Billing integrity is part of the profession's ethics, so I never inflate or misdescribe time.\"\n\nTip: Record substantive work contemporaneously with honest, specific descriptions — billing integrity is an ethical duty, not just an admin task.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why record time contemporaneously rather than reconstructing it later?","It's far more accurate and honest — reconstructed time is prone to error and padding, which harms the client and breaches billing ethics."]}],
["Client", "How do you conduct a client intake or initial information-gathering?", "**Why they ask:** paralegals often run intake under attorney supervision.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I gather the facts and documents the attorney needs systematically — the client's story, the timeline, the parties involved, relevant documents, and the deadlines or urgent issues — using an intake checklist so nothing's missed. I listen well and ask clear follow-up questions, because clients don't always know what's legally relevant. Crucially, I stay within my role: I gather information and explain the process, but I don't give legal advice or opinions on the merits, which only the attorney does. I also make sure a conflicts check is run before the relationship proceeds. Good intake gets the attorney a complete, organized picture so they can advise efficiently.\"\n\nTip: Gather facts, timeline, parties, documents, and deadlines via a checklist; stay within role (no legal advice), and ensure a conflicts check runs.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why run a conflicts check before taking on a client?","To ensure the firm isn't representing an adverse party or has another conflict — proceeding without checking risks an ethical violation and disqualification."]}],
["Professionalism", "How do you interact with clients professionally, especially difficult or distressed ones?", "**Why they ask:** paralegals are often the client's main day-to-day contact.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Many clients are going through something stressful — a lawsuit, a loss, a business crisis — so I'm patient, responsive, and clear, which reassures them even when I can't change the situation. I keep them updated on their matter's status and respond promptly, because silence breeds anxiety and complaints. With a difficult or emotional client I stay calm and empathetic without over-promising, and I'm careful to route anything that's a legal question or a decision to the attorney rather than offering reassurance I'm not authorized to give. Being the steady, organized, communicative point of contact is a huge part of the client's experience of the firm.\"\n\nTip: Be patient, responsive, and clear; keep clients updated, stay calm with distressed ones, and route legal questions to the attorney.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What do you do when a client asks you a direct legal question?","Acknowledge it and route it to the attorney rather than answering — giving legal advice is outside the paralegal role and could be unauthorized practice."]}],
["Teamwork", "How do you work effectively with the attorneys who supervise you?", "**Why they ask:** the supervising-attorney relationship defines the role.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I make the attorney's job easier by being reliable, anticipating what they'll need, and communicating clearly. I take direction well, ask clarifying questions up front rather than guessing and redoing work, and confirm deadlines and expectations. I keep them informed of status and flag problems early — a looming deadline, a document I can't locate — rather than letting them discover it late. Different attorneys have different styles and preferences, so I learn how each likes to work. And I understand the line: I support their work and exercise real skill within it, but the legal judgment and the client relationship are ultimately theirs.\"\n\nTip: Be reliable, anticipate needs, take direction and clarify up front, flag problems early, and adapt to each attorney's style within the supervised role.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why clarify instructions before starting rather than after?","Guessing wrong means redoing the work and wasting time on a deadline — a clarifying question up front is far cheaper than a redo."]}],
["Research", "How do you use legal research databases effectively?", "**Why they ask:** efficient database research is a practical daily skill.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I use platforms like the major legal research services to find cases, statutes, regulations, and secondary sources. The skill is efficiency: starting with a secondary source like a treatise or practice guide to get oriented fast, crafting focused searches with the right terms and filters rather than reading everything, and using citators to confirm authority is still good law. I keep track of what I've found and its relevance so I can report it clearly to the attorney. And I stay within my role — I gather and organize the authorities and summarize what they say, but the attorney draws the legal conclusions and decides how to use them.\"\n\nTip: Start with secondary sources, craft focused searches, confirm good law with citators, and report findings clearly — gathering authority, not drawing legal conclusions.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why start research with a secondary source?","Treatises and practice guides orient you quickly to the key authorities and framework, so your case-law searches are targeted rather than a blind hunt."]}],
["Discovery", "How do you draft and manage discovery requests and responses?", "**Why they ask:** discovery drafting and tracking is heavy paralegal work.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I draft discovery — interrogatories, requests for production, requests for admission — from templates and the attorney's strategy, tailored to the case's facts and claims, and following the rules on number and scope. On the responding side, I organize the gathering of responsive documents and information, help prepare responses and objections for attorney review, and track everything in a discovery log so I know what's outstanding, what's been produced, and what deadlines loom. Discovery has strict deadlines and rules, so I manage the calendar tightly and make sure productions are complete and properly formatted. The attorney sets strategy and finalizes objections; I run the machine that executes it.\"\n\nTip: Draft from templates and strategy within the rules, organize responses for attorney review, and track outstanding items and deadlines in a discovery log.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why keep a discovery log?","To track what's been requested, produced, and still outstanding, and the deadlines for each — so nothing is missed and the attorney always knows the status."]}],
["Specialty", "What does a transactional or corporate paralegal do differently from a litigation paralegal?", "**Why they ask:** paralegals specialize, and they want to gauge your area and adaptability.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Transactional work isn't about disputes — it's supporting deals and ongoing business. A corporate paralegal might handle entity formation and filings, maintain corporate records and minute books, support due diligence in an acquisition by organizing and reviewing documents, prepare closing checklists and binders, and manage signature pages and post-closing filings. The skills overlap — organization, drafting from templates, attention to detail, deadline management — but the rhythm differs: deals build to a closing rather than a trial, and the documents are agreements and filings rather than pleadings and discovery. I adapt the same core discipline to whatever the practice area needs.\"\n\nTip: Transactional/corporate paralegals support deals — formations, records, due diligence, closing checklists and binders — using the same core skills in a deal rhythm.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What's a closing checklist and why does it matter?","It lists every document and action required to close a deal, tracking what's signed and outstanding — it ensures nothing is missed before and after closing."]}],
["Specialty", "What are common paralegal tasks in estate planning or probate?", "**Why they ask:** probate and estates is a large paralegal practice area.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"In estate planning, a paralegal drafts documents like wills, trusts, and powers of attorney from the attorney's instructions and firm templates, gathers the client's asset and family information, and keeps everything organized for the attorney's review and the client's signing. In probate, the work involves administering an estate after death: preparing and filing probate petitions and court documents, inventorying and valuing assets, notifying heirs and creditors, tracking deadlines, and preparing accountings. It's detail- and deadline-heavy and involves sensitive family situations, so accuracy and tact both matter. As always, the attorney provides the legal advice and makes the judgment calls; I handle the drafting, organization, and process.\"\n\nTip: Estate planning: draft wills/trusts/POAs and gather info; probate: file petitions, inventory assets, notify heirs/creditors, track deadlines and accountings.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why is tact especially important in probate work?","You're dealing with grieving families and sometimes conflict over an estate, so sensitivity alongside accuracy and deadline discipline is part of doing the job well."]}],
["Specialty", "What does an immigration paralegal typically handle?", "**Why they ask:** immigration is a high-volume, form- and deadline-driven paralegal field.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Immigration paralegal work is heavily form- and process-driven: preparing visa, green card, and other applications and petitions, assembling the extensive supporting documentation each requires, tracking the many deadlines and processing timelines, and keeping meticulous case files. Accuracy is critical because errors or missing documents cause delays, denials, or requests for evidence that set a client back months. Clients are often anxious about high-stakes outcomes, so clear communication and reliability matter. The paralegal prepares and organizes everything to exacting standards, while the attorney provides the legal strategy and advice — I don't counsel clients on the law or their options, I execute the process precisely.\"\n\nTip: Immigration paralegals prepare form-heavy applications, assemble documentation, and track deadlines meticulously — accuracy prevents costly delays and denials.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why is documentation accuracy so critical in immigration cases?","Missing or incorrect documents trigger delays, requests for evidence, or denials that can set a client back months on a high-stakes outcome."]}]
]);

// Paralegal / Legal Assistant — part 3. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["lg-paralegal"] = (QB["lg-paralegal"] || []).concat([
["Privilege", "Explain attorney-client privilege and the work-product doctrine in practical terms.", "**Why they ask:** protecting privilege is a daily paralegal responsibility.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Attorney-client privilege protects confidential communications between a lawyer and client made for legal advice, so they generally can't be forced into disclosure. Work product protects materials prepared in anticipation of litigation — an attorney's or team's notes, analysis, and strategy. Practically, it shapes how I handle documents: I'm careful about who's copied on sensitive communications, because looping in the wrong person can waive privilege, and I flag potentially privileged material during document review so it isn't accidentally produced. I don't make the final privilege determination — that's the attorney's call — but I understand the concepts well enough to protect them and to spot documents that need the attorney's review.\"\n\nTip: Privilege protects confidential legal-advice communications; work product protects litigation-prep materials — handle sensitive docs carefully to avoid waiver, and flag for attorney review.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["How can privilege be accidentally waived?","By sharing a privileged communication with someone outside the privilege — copying a third party, or forwarding it broadly — which can destroy the protection."]}],
["Confidentiality", "How do you handle redaction and protect sensitive information in document productions?", "**Why they ask:** improper redaction and inadvertent disclosure are serious, common errors.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Redaction has to be done properly and verified, because a botched redaction — text that's merely hidden visually but still extractable from the file — is a real and recurring disclosure disaster. So I redact using tools that actually remove the underlying data, then I verify by checking the produced file, not just the on-screen view. For sensitive information like personal identifiers, I follow the applicable rules and any protective order on what must be redacted or marked confidential. Before any production goes out, I double-check the set for privileged or sensitive material that shouldn't be in it, because once documents are produced, you can't fully un-ring that bell.\"\n\nTip: Redact so the underlying data is actually removed and verify it; follow rules and protective orders; double-check productions before they go out — disclosure is often irreversible.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why is a visually-hidden redaction dangerous?","If the text is only covered on screen but still in the file, the recipient can extract it — the sensitive data was never actually removed, causing a real breach."]}],
["Priorities", "How do you handle competing deadlines and demands from multiple attorneys?", "**Why they ask:** paralegals routinely juggle several supervisors and matters.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I start by getting the real priorities straight rather than just reacting to whoever asked last or loudest. I look at the actual deadlines and stakes, and when two urgent things genuinely collide, I don't silently pick one and let the other slip — I communicate proactively with the attorneys about the conflict so they can help prioritize or adjust, because they may know one deadline has flexibility and the other doesn't. I keep a clear task list with due dates, build in buffer, and give realistic timelines rather than over-promising. Managing up with honest communication is the key skill; the failure mode is quietly missing something because I didn't flag that I was overloaded.\"\n\nTip: Prioritize by deadline and stakes, surface genuine conflicts to the attorneys early, keep a clear task list, and give realistic timelines — don't silently let something slip.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What do you do when two urgent deadlines truly conflict?","Raise it with both attorneys promptly so they can reprioritize or shift a flexible deadline — they often have information about urgency that resolves the conflict."]}],
["Accountability", "Tell me about a time you made a mistake. How did you handle it?", "**Why they ask:** everyone errs; they want honesty and the right response under high stakes.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"I once sent a document to the wrong recipient in an email — caught it quickly, but it had already gone. I didn't hide it; I immediately told the supervising attorney exactly what happened so they could assess any privilege or confidentiality impact and take steps like requesting its return. Then I looked at how it happened and changed my own process — double-checking recipients before sending anything sensitive, and using tools that confirm addresses. In legal work a hidden mistake is far worse than the mistake itself, because the attorney needs to know in time to mitigate. Owning it fast and fixing the process is the only right response.\"\n\nTip: Own mistakes immediately so the attorney can mitigate, then fix the process — a hidden error is far worse than the error itself.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why report a mistake immediately rather than trying to quietly fix it?","The attorney may need to act fast to mitigate harm — like requesting a document's return before it's read — and can only do that if they know in time."]}],
["Accuracy", "How do you ensure accuracy and catch errors in your work?", "**Why they ask:** in legal work small errors have outsized consequences.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I build checking into the process rather than relying on a single final glance. I proofread carefully, and for anything important I review it again with fresh eyes after a break, since you miss your own errors when you read what you meant to write. I use checklists for repeatable tasks like filings and closings so no step is forgotten, cross-check names, dates, numbers, and citations against the source, and read defined terms and cross-references to make sure they're consistent. For critical documents I'll have a second person review when possible. The mindset is that in legal work 'mostly right' can be a serious problem, so accuracy is worth the extra pass.\"\n\nTip: Build checking into the process — fresh-eyes review, checklists, cross-checking against sources, and a second reviewer for critical work.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why review important documents with fresh eyes after a break?","You read what you intended rather than what's on the page right after writing — a pause lets you catch errors your brain was auto-correcting."]}],
["Procedure", "What is service of process and why does it matter?", "**Why they ask:** proper service is a procedural requirement paralegals often coordinate.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Service of process is formally delivering legal documents — like a summons and complaint — to a party so they have legal notice of an action, done according to the rules on who can serve, how, and on whom. It matters because a court generally can't proceed against a party that wasn't properly served; defective service can get a case delayed or dismissed, or a default judgment later overturned. As a paralegal I coordinate service — arranging a process server, preparing the documents, tracking that it was accomplished within the deadline, and obtaining and filing the proof of service. Getting it right is foundational; a case can be undermined before it even starts by improper service.\"\n\nTip: Service gives a party legal notice per strict rules; defective service can delay or undo a case — coordinate it, meet the deadline, and file proof of service.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What's proof of service and why file it?","It's the document confirming service was properly made; filing it establishes for the court that the party received legal notice, which the case depends on."]}],
["Procedure", "How do you handle preparing or responding to a subpoena?", "**Why they ask:** subpoenas involve procedure, deadlines, and sometimes third parties.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"On the issuing side, I help prepare a subpoena — for documents or testimony — making sure it meets the formal requirements, identifies what's sought clearly, and is served properly on the recipient with the required notice. On the receiving side, when the firm or a client is subpoenaed, I calendar the response deadline immediately, help gather responsive materials, and flag anything privileged or objectionable for the attorney, who decides on objections or a motion to quash. Subpoenas often involve third parties and strict timelines, so I track them carefully and make sure we respond or object in time rather than ignoring one, which can have real consequences.\"\n\nTip: Prepare subpoenas to meet formal requirements and serve properly; on receipt, calendar the deadline, gather materials, and flag privilege/objections for the attorney.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What happens if a subpoena is ignored?","It can lead to contempt or sanctions — so even a subpoena you intend to object to requires a timely response or motion rather than silence."]}],
["Analysis", "How do you summarize voluminous records like medical or financial documents?", "**Why they ask:** condensing large record sets is a high-value paralegal deliverable.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The goal is to turn a thick stack into something the attorney can actually use. For medical records in an injury case, I'd build a chronological summary — dates of treatment, providers, diagnoses, procedures, and key findings — with references back to the page so the attorney can jump to the source. For financial records, I'd organize transactions or entries to show the relevant pattern. I stay factual and neutral, flagging gaps or notable items, and I'm careful not to interpret or draw conclusions that are the attorney's or an expert's to make — a medical summary reports what the records say, it doesn't diagnose. Accurate, organized, page-referenced summaries save the attorney enormous time.\"\n\nTip: Build chronological, page-referenced, factual summaries flagging key items and gaps — organize and report, don't interpret or conclude.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why keep a records summary neutral and factual?","Interpreting medical or financial significance is for the attorney or an expert — the summary's job is to accurately organize and reference what the records actually say."]}],
["Litigation", "How do you help prepare a settlement demand or mediation package?", "**Why they ask:** most cases settle, and demand packages are substantive paralegal work.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A demand package has to tell the case's story persuasively and back it with evidence, so I help assemble it under the attorney's direction: organizing the liability facts, the supporting documents and exhibits, the records of damages — medical bills, wage loss, repair costs — and often a chronology that makes the narrative clear. I make sure the supporting evidence is complete, well-organized, and accurately summarized, and that the numbers add up and are documented. The attorney crafts the legal arguments and the demand itself; I build the organized, evidence-backed foundation that makes it credible. A well-assembled package can drive a better settlement by showing the other side we're prepared and the claim is solid.\"\n\nTip: Assemble the liability facts, exhibits, documented damages, and a clear chronology under attorney direction — a well-organized, evidence-backed package drives better settlements.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does organization matter in a demand package?","A clear, complete, well-documented package signals the case is strong and the firm is prepared, which pressures the other side toward a fair settlement."]}],
["Duties", "What should a paralegal know about notarizing documents?", "**Why they ask:** many paralegals are also notaries and must understand the duties.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"If I'm a commissioned notary, notarizing means verifying the signer's identity, confirming they're signing willingly and appear to understand, and that they're signing in my presence, then completing the notarial certificate and journal properly. The core duty is that a notary is an impartial witness to the signing and identity — I don't verify the content or give any opinion on the document, and I never notarize a signature I didn't witness or for someone whose identity I can't confirm, no matter how routine it seems or who asks. Notarizing improperly, like pre-signing a certificate or notarizing for an absent signer, is a serious violation. I follow my jurisdiction's notary rules precisely.\"\n\nTip: A notary impartially verifies identity, willingness, and in-person signing — never notarize an unwitnessed signature or unconfirmed identity, whatever the pressure.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why never notarize a signature you didn't witness?","The notarization certifies the person signed in your presence; doing it for an absent signer is fraud on the notarial act and a serious violation, even if 'everyone knows' it's them."]}],
["Security", "How do you protect client confidentiality in a digital, sometimes-remote environment?", "**Why they ask:** data security is now inseparable from the confidentiality duty.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The confidentiality duty hasn't changed, but the ways to breach it have multiplied. I follow the firm's security practices: strong authentication, encrypted channels for sensitive material, not using personal or unsecured devices and networks for client data, locking my screen, and being alert to phishing that targets law firms for exactly this information. Working remotely I'm careful about who can see my screen or overhear calls, and I keep physical documents secure. I also don't discuss matters where I can be overheard, in person or online. Client information is a trust, and in a digital environment protecting it means being deliberate about the technology, not just discreet in conversation.\"\n\nTip: Apply the confidentiality duty to tech — strong auth, encryption, secure devices/networks, phishing awareness, and privacy when working remotely.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why are law firms a frequent phishing target?","They hold concentrated sensitive client and matter information, so attackers target them specifically — making security awareness part of the confidentiality duty."]}],
["Development", "How do paralegal certifications and continuing education fit your career?", "**Why they ask:** professional development signals commitment to the field.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Paralegal certifications offered by the national professional associations demonstrate competence and commitment and can strengthen credibility, and some paralegals pursue them to validate their skills and advance. Continuing education matters because the law, the rules, and especially the technology keep changing — e-discovery tools and now AI-assisted tools are reshaping the work. I stay current through CLE-type offerings, association resources, and learning new systems the firm adopts, and I specialize deeper in my practice area while keeping my core skills sharp. Treating it as an ongoing profession rather than just a job is what distinguishes paralegals who grow into senior, trusted roles.\"\n\nTip: Certifications validate competence; ongoing education keeps pace with changing law, rules, and technology — treat it as a profession, not just a job.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's a current area worth learning for paralegals?","Legal technology — e-discovery platforms and emerging AI-assisted tools — since they're reshaping document review and research and are increasingly expected skills."]}],
["Ethics", "What would you do if an attorney or client asked you to do something improper?", "**Why they ask:** paralegals face ethical pressure and must know their limits.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd decline to do the improper thing and raise my concern professionally. If it's something like backdating a document, misrepresenting a date to a court, altering a record, or helping conceal something, I won't do it regardless of who asks, because it's unethical and can expose the client, the firm, and me — and 'I was told to' isn't a defense. I'd first assume good faith and clarify, since sometimes there's a misunderstanding, but if it's genuinely improper I hold the line respectfully and, if needed, escalate to a supervising attorney or the firm's ethics resources. My professional integrity isn't something I'll trade to avoid an awkward moment.\"\n\nTip: Decline the improper act regardless of who asks, clarify in case of misunderstanding, and escalate if needed — 'I was told to' is not a defense.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why isn't following an instruction a defense to an ethical violation?","You're responsible for your own professional conduct — knowingly participating in something improper exposes you personally, whatever you were told to do."]}],
["Time", "How do you stay productive and calm during a high-pressure crunch, like a trial or closing week?", "**Why they ask:** legal work has intense peaks and they want to see you handle them.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I rely on preparation and system rather than adrenaline. Going into a crunch I make sure the organization is already solid, so I'm executing a plan, not inventing one under pressure. During it I prioritize ruthlessly — the must-dos for the deadline first — keep a running checklist so nothing's forgotten in the chaos, and communicate clearly with the team about status. I stay calm because panic causes errors, and errors under deadline are the worst kind. I also know when to ask for help rather than quietly drowning. The crunches are when paralegal value is most visible, so I treat them as the job working as intended, not an emergency.\"\n\nTip: Prepare ahead so you execute a plan, prioritize ruthlessly, keep a checklist, communicate status, stay calm, and ask for help — panic causes errors.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why is staying calm under deadline pressure a work skill, not just temperament?","Panic causes exactly the errors that are most costly under deadline — a calm, systematic approach protects accuracy when it matters most."]}],
["Independence", "How do you work effectively and independently with limited supervision?", "**Why they ask:** experienced paralegals must self-manage, especially remotely.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I take ownership of my matters and move them forward without needing to be told each step, while staying clear on where my authority ends. That means anticipating what's next on a case, hitting deadlines without reminders, and making sound judgment calls within my role — and recognizing the moments that genuinely need the attorney's decision rather than guessing. I keep the attorney informed with concise updates so they trust the work is handled without having to check on it, which is what earns more autonomy. Independence isn't doing whatever I want; it's being reliably self-directed on the execution while routing the real legal judgment up.\"\n\nTip: Own your matters and move them forward reliably, make sound calls within your role, route genuine legal judgment up, and keep the attorney concisely informed.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["How do you earn more autonomy from a supervising attorney?","By being consistently reliable and keeping them informed so they trust the work is handled — demonstrated dependability is what loosens the oversight."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a time you went above and beyond on a matter.", "**Why they ask:** they want initiative and dedication, not just task completion.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"On a case with a massive, disorganized document production coming in days before a deadline, I saw the attorney was going to be buried. Beyond organizing it, I built a focused index and chronology of the key documents and flagged the ones that looked most significant to our theory, so the attorney could go straight to what mattered instead of wading through thousands of pages. It wasn't strictly asked of me, but it saved critical time and the attorney said it materially helped their prep. I try to think about what the attorney actually needs to succeed, not just the literal task — that's where a paralegal adds the most value.\"\n\nTip: Show initiative that anticipated the attorney's real need and materially helped — thinking beyond the literal task is where paralegals add the most value.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What mindset leads to adding value beyond the assigned task?","Thinking about what the attorney actually needs to succeed on the matter, not just the literal instruction — anticipating the next need before being asked."]}],
["Quality", "How do you maintain quality during large-volume document review?", "**Why they ask:** review volume can tempt shortcuts that cause real harm.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Volume is where discipline matters most, because a rushed review can miss a key document or produce a privileged one. I follow the review protocol consistently — the agreed criteria for relevance and privilege — rather than drifting as fatigue sets in, use the platform's tools like search, grouping, and deduplication to work smarter, and take breaks to keep my judgment sharp, since error rates climb when you're tired and skimming. For privilege calls I err toward flagging for attorney review when unsure rather than guessing. And I raise questions about ambiguous categories early so the whole review stays consistent. Speed matters, but a missed privileged document in a production is the kind of error that can't be undone.\"\n\nTip: Follow the review protocol consistently, use platform tools, manage fatigue, flag uncertain privilege calls up, and raise ambiguities early — consistency over speed.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why flag uncertain privilege calls rather than deciding them yourself?","A wrongly produced privileged document can waive privilege irreversibly — when unsure, routing it to the attorney protects against an unrecoverable error."]}],
["Professionalism", "What do you need to know about professionalism and etiquette when you're at court?", "**Why they ask:** paralegals represent the firm in court settings.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"At court I'm representing the firm, so I dress and conduct myself professionally, arrive early, and know the specific court's procedures and personnel. I'm respectful to court staff — clerks and the judge's staff can make a day go smoothly or not, and courtesy matters as well as being right. I stay organized and ready so I can hand the attorney anything they need instantly, and I'm discreet, never discussing the case where it can be overheard, including in hallways and elevators where the other side or jurors might be. I understand my role in the courtroom and stay within it. How the whole team carries itself reflects on the firm and can affect how the case is received.\"\n\nTip: Dress and act professionally, arrive early, respect court staff, stay organized and ready, and be discreet everywhere — you represent the firm.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why be careful about conversations in courthouse hallways?","Opposing parties, witnesses, or jurors could overhear case discussion — discretion everywhere in the building protects confidentiality and the case."]}],
["Technology", "How do you adapt to new legal technology, including AI-assisted tools?", "**Why they ask:** technology is reshaping paralegal work and adaptability is valued.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I treat new tools as leverage, not a threat, while keeping a clear head about their limits. E-discovery platforms, document automation, and newer AI-assisted research or review tools can make me far more efficient, so I invest in learning them well. But I apply judgment: AI tools can be wrong or produce plausible-looking but inaccurate output, so I verify what they generate rather than trusting it blindly — a fabricated citation or a missed nuance can cause real harm, and the responsibility for accuracy stays with the human. I also stay mindful of confidentiality when using any tool with client data. Used carefully, technology lets me spend more time on the higher-value judgment and organization that actually need a person.\"\n\nTip: Learn new tools as leverage but verify AI output for accuracy and confidentiality — responsibility for correctness stays with the human.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why verify output from an AI research tool?","It can produce plausible but wrong results, including fabricated citations — relying on it unchecked can harm the client and the firm, so a human must confirm accuracy."]}],
["Closing", "What makes a great paralegal?", "**Why they ask:** self-awareness about the role's real value.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Exceptional organization and attention to detail, because the role runs on accuracy and not dropping things; reliability under deadline, since attorneys need to trust the work is handled; and sound judgment about where the paralegal role ends and the attorney's begins. Beyond the technical skills, a great paralegal anticipates what the attorney and case need rather than just completing assigned tasks, communicates clearly, stays calm under pressure, and holds the line on ethics and confidentiality. The best ones become genuinely indispensable — the person who keeps the matters running, catches the things others miss, and lets the attorneys focus on the law. That trusted, anticipatory reliability is the whole game.\"\n\nTip: Organization and accuracy, deadline reliability, sound judgment on the role's limits, anticipation, and ethics — indispensable, anticipatory reliability.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["What distinguishes a senior paralegal from a junior one?","Anticipating needs and exercising sound judgment within the role — moving matters forward reliably with minimal supervision, and catching what others miss."]}]
]);
